\section{Bounding a Queue Changes When a Write Can Finish}

\Cref{ch:ext-355} gives processes blocking reads and semantically unbounded FIFO channels. The survey by Johnston, Hanna, and Millar describes the mismatch between unbounded arcs and finite token storage and notes that the implementation can deadlock where the abstract model would not \cite{johnston2004dataflow}. This matters after \Cref{ch:ext-381}: a valid streaming specialization can still be embedded in a runtime that introduces a new wait. A capacity bound needs a justification for the chosen schedule or an explicit change to the source contract.

\section{Diagnose a Deadlock Introduced by Capacity One}

Use two single-producer, single-consumer channels, \texttt{data} and \texttt{ready}. A producer writes \(7\) and \(9\) to \texttt{data} and then writes a marker to \texttt{ready}. The consumer reads the marker first and then reads two data values. This program is not polling either channel. The producer can finish its writes on unbounded channels regardless of when the consumer first tries to read. With one data slot and ordinary blocking writes, the producer cannot enqueue \(9\). The consumer cannot read \(7\) until the producer publishes the marker. Making the marker queue larger does not help; at deadlock it is empty.

\Cref{fig:visual-384} distinguishes the waits from the data transfer edges: a write waits for space, and a read waits for a token. This cycle follows from the concrete process positions and capacities, not from the existence of a cycle in the static actor graph. In fact both of the program's data channels point in the same direction.

