\section{Replay a Chosen Schedule With a Bound}

For a fixed schedule over a closed fixture, record a peak, then replay with the proposed allocation. Reject a step if its precondition fails instead of quietly deferring it and accepting a different schedule. The proof is then about this order of atomic operations, not all possible process interleavings.

\begin{verbatim}
replay(operations, capacity):
    queues := empty_queues()
    peak := zero_per_channel()
    for operation in operations:
        q := queues[operation.channel]
        if operation is Write(value):
            if q.count >= capacity[q.id]:
                return BlockedWrite(operation, q.count)
            q.push(value)
            peak[q.id] := max(peak[q.id], q.count)
        else if operation is Read(expected):
            if q.empty(): return BlockedRead(operation)
            require q.pop() == expected
    return peak
\end{verbatim}

In the successful replay, the writes to \texttt{data} precede the marker write, then the marker read precedes both data reads. The peak vector is two data tokens and one marker. Order matters more than the sum of peaks: swapping those capacities still blocks the second data write. If the implementation can schedule dynamically, explore reachable states with the intended capacities and collect states where unfinished processes cannot step. Include externally blocked states in the result, but distinguish them from internal deadlock by identifying which external event could make progress possible. Bounded exploration of a finite fixture is evidence for that fixture, not for arbitrary programs.

\section{Choose What Backpressure Means at the Boundary}

A strictly synchronous driver might prevent the next external arrival until the consumer acknowledges the current batch. A continuous producer may instead require a capacity proof, a documented drop policy, or memory that can grow. Dropping or reordering data is not a transparent implementation of unbounded FIFO semantics. A library that exposes backpressure can make it part of its API; clients then reason about which events must drain buffers and what a failed write means. Test both progress and the transferred sequence \(7,9\), including a stalled consumer. Success on small queues is not just reaching termination: it is reaching termination with the promised observations intact.

\section{Explore Schedule Choices for the Finite Fixture}

The replay accepts one schedule. For the finite processes above we
can do more: enumerate every reachable pair of instruction pointers
and queue contents under each allocation. The initial state has empty
queues and both processes at their first instruction. A successor
advances just one enabled process. Retain concrete queue contents in
the state so the same exploration can check the consumer's reads.

\begin{verbatim}
visit(state):
    if state in seen: return
    add state to seen
    if all processes complete:
        require state.consumer_values == [7, 9]
        return
    next := legal_one_process_steps(state)
    if next is empty:
        report_stuck(state, blocked_operations(state))
    for successor in next:
        visit(successor)
\end{verbatim}

With capacity one, the stuck state has exactly one queued value, no
marker and neither process at completion. With capacity two, every
nonterminal state of this fixture has at least one legal successor.
There are no cycles because the instruction pointers only move
forward. The same test for a looping network also needs a progress
criterion: a run can continue forever without delivering a required
output. A deadlock-free result alone would not rule that out.
